# Build context exclusions for the WEB image.
#
# The root Dockerfile does `COPY . .`, so without this file every build of
# `web` copies the whole project into the image -- including signer_service/
# and any Turnkey credentials in it. That would put the signing key inside
# the one container that must never have it, silently, in an image layer
# that persists even after the file is later deleted from disk.
#
# The signer is a SEPARATE service with its own build context
# (build: ./signer_service) and its own .dockerignore. The web image has no
# use for any of it.
signer_service/

# Secrets. The web container receives these as environment variables at
# RUNTIME via docker-compose; baking them into an image layer is both
# unnecessary and permanent.
.env
*.env
!.env.example
# Any file whose name mentions credentials, secrets or keys, in ANY
# format. The earlier patterns were anchored to .json and missed a
# turnkey-credentials.TXT that appeared later -- an extension is not a
# security boundary.
*credential*
*credentials*
*secret*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*

# Not needed at runtime, and .git in particular can be large and carries
# full history of anything ever committed.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
pgdata/

# Local scratch / docs that don't belong in a runtime image.
*.md
!README.md
error.txt

# Operational artefacts -- never runtime inputs, and each one baked into an
# image layer is both permanent and a cache-buster: every report run changed
# the build context, so every rebuild re-ran `COPY . .` from scratch.
# run*.txt / web-crash.txt hold snapshots of live measurements and logs.
run*.txt
web-crash.txt
*.log
*.bak
*.orig
_to_delete/
# .env variants. `.env` and `*.env` do not match `.env.bak` or `.env.local`,
# which is exactly the file someone makes before editing the real one.
.env.*
!.env.example
